The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!ns->opt[i + 1]) {
    kfree_skb(reply);
    return;
}
if (ns->opt[i] == ND_OPT_SOURCE_LL_ADDR) {
    daddr = ns->opt + i + sizeof(struct nd_opt_hdr);
    break;
}
```
Reason:
The first statement only checks if the option length is zero (invalid), but does not verify that `ns->opt[i + 1] << 3` (the option's stated length in bytes) does not exceed the remaining `ns_olen`. A malformed option with a large length can cause the loop to advance past the available option data, leading to out-of-bounds reads in subsequent iterations or when reading the source LLADDR. Additionally, the second statement reads the source LLADDR payload assuming the option is at least `sizeof(struct nd_opt_hdr) + ETH_ALEN` bytes, but without validation, a short option (e.g., only 1 byte after the header) could cause an out-of-bounds read of `ns->opt` when copying the Ethernet address.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (!ns->opt[i + 1] || i + (ns->opt[i + 1] << 3) > ns_olen) {
    kfree_skb(reply);
    return;
}
if (ns->opt[i] == ND_OPT_SOURCE_LL_ADDR) {
    if ((ns->opt[i + 1] << 3) >=
        sizeof(struct nd_opt_hdr) + ETH_ALEN)
        daddr = ns->opt + i + sizeof(struct nd_opt_hdr);
    break;
}
```
Reason:
The first condition now also checks that `i + (ns->opt[i + 1] << 3) > ns_olen` — i.e., the option's claimed length does not extend beyond the total NS option area. This prevents the parser from advancing beyond the safe buffer bounds. The second change adds a guard before reading the source LLADDR: it ensures the option length (in bytes) is at least `sizeof(struct nd_opt_hdr) + ETH_ALEN` (the minimum needed to hold the Ethernet address). If the option is shorter, the source address `daddr` is not updated, and the function falls back to using the Ethernet source address from the request (set earlier). This avoids using a truncated or out-of-bounds LLADDR.

